    {
      "type": "textEditor",
      "data": {
        "value": "<table border=\"1\" cellspacing=\"1\" cellpadding=\"1\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Test Name</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Results</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Units</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Bio. Ref Intervel</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>HLA- B5 (51/52), DISEASE ASSOCIATION, WHOLE BLOOD<br>(SSOP- Luminex)</td>\n<td>B5 <strong>Positive for 51</strong> &amp; Negative for 52&nbsp;</td>\n<td>&nbsp;</td>\n<td>&nbsp;</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><br><strong>COMMENTS</strong></p>\n<p>HLA B5 has two splits B*51 and B*52 of which <strong>only </strong>B*51 is important in the etiology of Behcet&rsquo;s disease. HLA-B*51 and B*52 have almost similar distribution in Indian population ranging from 6.8-15% in various ethnic groups in the country. Ocular involvement, which could lead to serious complications, is a very important feature of Behcet&rsquo;s disease. HLA-B*51 positivity increases the risk for Behcet&rsquo;s disease by six times. HLA-B*52 is not associated with additional risk.</p>",
        "field": "textEditor"
      },
